hi I'm Annie whoo and I'm a mathematics  teacher one of the things that I love  most in the current sort of educational  milieu is the real recognition through  work of people like Carol Dweck and Joe  bola about the growth mindset and how  important it is to give messages to our  students about the importance of  struggle and the ability for them to  grow in their capacity to actually  change their brains which i think is  amazing but I think often we stop at  applying it to students and we don't  realize we also need to apply it to  ourselves as teacher that's all  professional learning is is the  application of a growth mindset to us as  educators and I think it's really  important that you know as a leader of  no 2010 mathematics teachers in my  school they all come from very different  backgrounds and I want them to be able  to wherever they are coming from to grow  and I think it's also important to not  think about this in two in linear terms  like some people are at 30% and some  people are at 75 and I just want to move  them forward even though I do want to  move them forward I think people need to  be moving outward I think actually  growth is the perfect metaphor because  you know you have a look at different  plants different trees and they're all  going to grow in different ways some  vines are amazing as spreading out over  this huge different you know space of  some trees just go straight up and  they're just amazing the redwoods in  these forests that are hundreds of years  old everyone comes with a different  background different skill set and I  think cultivating that diversity and  recognizing those strengths that are  unique to among my staff people who've  come into teaching from industry people  who maybe came from primer or a  different subject people who have just  been all-in with maths their entire  lives every single one of them brings a  different set of skills and and their  ability to teach a diverse range of  students actually hinges on their  ability to to maximise on the strengths  that they have to take those spots where  they perhaps may be naturally weak and  sort of bolster them with the skills of  their colleagues I think that's why team  teaching is such a powerful mechanism to  use in the classroom because there's  nothing quite like even though you may  talk about things in the staff room and  say oh that's a great idea I'll bring  that into my my classroom it's totally  different to see it actually work live  even if it  take sixty kids in the room to have to  make that happen I I love seeing the way  other teachers just speak and interact  with kids and the questions that they  ask things like that there's no  substitute to watching it live and to  even be able to interact with us so I  think those kinds of strategies for  helping our teachers have those  conversations about improvement but also  see how it can happen and see how they  can grow in a variety of ways in the  classroom I think that that's our  responsibility as mathematics leaders  [Music]
 

